<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolesław V the Chaste</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1226">1226</a>–<a href="/years/1279">1279</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Boleslaw V the Chaste</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolesław V the Chaste was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and Sandomierz from 1243. His reign saw repeated Mongol invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>, in 1241 and 1259 to 1260. He granted Kraków town rights in 1257 and supported the salt mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wieliczka">Wieliczka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bochnia">Bochnia</a>. He died childless, and his lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-black">Leszek the Black</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boleslaw-v-the-chaste.jpg" alt="Bolesław V the Chaste" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Zuber at Polish Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piecz%C4%99%C4%87_Boles%C5%82awa_Wstydliwego.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
